WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 180: Dwight D. Eisenhower: The Cold War, Interstate Highways, and the Little Rock Crisis (1953–1961)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.040
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our journey with Dwight D.

4
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.080
[Rebecca] Eisenhower.

5
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:21.920
[Rebecca] In the tense nuclear shadows of the 1950s, Eisenhower steered the United States through peace, historic civil rights confrontations, and an economic boom that built modern suburban America.

6
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:32.800
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the man everyone liked, the interstate highway miracle, and the warning against the military-industrial complex.

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:46.160
[George] By 1952, the American public was weary of 20 continuous years of democratic rule, traumatized by the bloody stalemate in Korea, and alarmed by communist expansion across Asia and Eastern Europe.

8
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.360
[George] Both political parties courted General Dwight D.

9
00:00:49.680 --> 00:01:02.400
[George] Eisenhower, but Ike chose the Republican nomination to save the party from its isolationist faction led by Senator Robert Taft, campaigning with his trademark ear-to-ear grin and infectious warmth.

10
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:10.320
[George] Eisenhower inspired an unprecedented grassroots cultural phenomenon under the simple, brilliant slogan, I like Ike.

11
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:17.840
[George] In October 1952, making the definitive pledge of the campaign, Eisenhower announced, I shall go to Korea.

12
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.160
[George] The nation believed him implicitly.

13
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:30.320
[George] In November, Eisenhower swept 39 states in an electoral landslide, capturing 34 million votes over Democratic candidate Adlai Stevenson.

14
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:44.320
[George] In December, true to his sacred promise, the president-elect secretly flew to the freezing front lines of Korea, eating rations with foot soldiers in combat trenches and assessing the battlefield with a professional warrior's eye.

15
00:01:44.320 --> 00:02:01.240
[George] Armed with that first hand knowledge, Eisenhower used backchannel diplomatic pressure and quiet nuclear deterrence to force communist negotiators back to the table at Panmunjom, securing a signed armistice in July 1953 that silenced the guns and ended the Korean War.

16
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:13.080
[George] In traveling to Korea, Eisenhower demonstrated the immense moral authority of a commander who refused to ask young American soldiers to endure hardships he was unwilling to witness himself.

17
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:22.760
[George] The armistice demonstrated that resolute national strength paired with quiet diplomacy could halt communist aggression without escalating into total atomic war.

18
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:32.520
[George] Eisenhower's most enduring peacetime monument was not carved in marble, but laid in four-lane concrete across 41,000 miles of American landscape.

19
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:39.560
[George] The vision had been planted 37 years earlier in 1919, when young Major Eisenhower accompanied the U.S.

20
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:52.360
[George] Army's first transcontinental truck convoy, a grueling, axle-breaking 62-day crawl from Washington to San Francisco over unpaved mud ruts, broken wooden bridges, and quicksand.

21
00:02:52.360 --> 00:03:05.080
[George] Later, during the Allied advance across Germany in 1945, Eisenhower had marveled at Hitler's high-speed Autobahn network, recognizing its revolutionary military and civilian utility.

22
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:17.160
[George] In June 1956, President Eisenhower signed the Federal Aid Highway Act, authorizing $25 billion for the largest public works project in the history of human civilization.

23
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.120
[George] The interstate highway system transformed American society down to its roots.

24
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:38.120
[George] It spurred the growth of modern suburbs, gave birth to roadside motels and drive in diners, enabled coast-to-coast trucking logistics, and allowed millions of ordinary families to pack their station wagons and explore the majestic breadth of the American continent.

25
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:49.520
[George] Designed with straightaways capable of serving as emergency landing strips for military aircraft, the interstate system unified the Republic as nothing had since the Transcontinental Railroad.

26
00:03:49.520 --> 00:04:01.440
[George] The Interstate Highway System became the indispensable circulatory system of modern American capitalism, linking remote farming communities to major industrial seaports in hours rather than weeks.

27
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:11.120
[George] The concrete freeways spurred an unprecedented wave of domestic travel, transforming small towns into thriving regional hubs of commerce and manufacturing.

28
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:18.320
[George] In May 1954, the United States Supreme Court delivered its unanimous, epicaling in Brown v.

29
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.160
[George] Board of Education, declaring racial segregation in public schools unconstitutional.

30
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:36.080
[George] Though Eisenhower was personally cautious about social change by judicial decree, he believed with religious fervor that his solemn constitutional oath compelled him to enforce federal law against mob rule.

31
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.360
[George] The ultimate test arrived in September 1957 in Little Rock, Arkansas.

32
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:54.720
[George] Governor Orville Fawbus, seeking political re-election, deployed the Arkansas National Guard to physically block nine courageous African American teenagers, the Little Rock Nine, from entering Central High School.

33
00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:02.400
[George] A screaming white mob hurled vile epithets, spit upon 15-year-old Elizabeth Eckford, and threatened violence.

34
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:12.160
[George] When Fawbus withdrew the state troops and left the school surrounded by an uncontrollable mob of 1,000 rioters, Eisenhower struck with iron military resolve.

35
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.960
[George] In a nationwide address, he announced mob rule cannot be allowed to override the decisions of our courts.

36
00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Eisenhower federalized the Arkansas National Guard, stripping Faubus of command, and dispatched 1,000 battle-hardened paratroopers of the 101st Airborne Division to Little Rock.

37
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:42.520
[George] With fixed bayonets gleaming in the Arkansas Sun, soldiers escorted the nine students through the front doors, establishing the supremacy of federal constitutional law over state nullification.

38
00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:54.920
[George] By deploying the 101st Airborne to Little Rock, Eisenhower made it unmistakably clear to the entire world that the United States would enforce constitutional equality at the point of a bayonet if necessary.

39
00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:05.720
[George] The decisive action established an unshakable legal boundary, proving that no individual governor or local faction could defy the constitutional rights of American citizens.

40
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:16.520
[George] Haunted by the horrific destructive capacity of the hydrogen bomb tests in the Pacific, Eisenhower sought tirelessly to steer nuclear technology toward the betterment of human life.

41
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:36.760
[George] In December 1953, he delivered his celebrated Atoms for Peace address before the United Nations General Assembly, proposing an international atomic energy agency to channel fissionable materials into clean electrical power, medical isotopes, and agricultural research rather than catastrophic weapons of war.

42
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:50.200
[George] But on October 4, 1957, the geopolitical sky fell when the Soviet Union successfully launched Sputnik 1, the world's first artificial satellite, beeping mockingly overhead every 90 minutes.

43
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:58.520
[George] A wave of public panic swept the United States, with critics charging that America had fallen dangerously behind Soviet science.

44
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.880
[George] Refusing to succumb to hysteria, Eisenhower acted with visionary deliberation.

45
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:28.080
[George] In 1958, he established the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, NASA, to oversee peaceful space exploration, signed the National Defense Education Act to pour billions into science and mathematics instruction, and created DARPA, the defense research agency that would later invent the ARPANET, the foundational forerunner of the modern Internet.

46
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:40.000
[George] The establishment of NASA and the educational renaissance that followed Sputnik proved that American democracy could respond to totalitarian scientific challenges with breathtaking civilian innovation.

47
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] The birth of the Civilian Space Agency galvanized generations of young scientists, leading to quantum leaps in telecommunications, computing, and satellite meteorology.

48
00:07:50.960 --> 00:08:00.480
[George] Throughout the late 1950s, Eisenhower maintained peace with the Soviet Union through a daring secret weapon, the Lockheed U-2 spy plane.

49
00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:10.800
[George] Flying at 70,000 feet, far above the reach of Soviet radar and interceptor fighters, the U-2 took high-resolution photographs of Soviet missile sites.

50
00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:19.920
[George] Proving conclusively to Eisenhower that Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev's boasts of turning out intercontinental ballistic missiles like sausages was a bluff.

51
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:27.520
[George] But on May 1, 1960, just two weeks before a crucial four-power peace summit in Paris, disaster struck.

52
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:36.960
[George] A Soviet surface-to-air missile detonated near a U-2 piloted by CIA operative Francis Gary Powers over Sverdlovsk, downing the plane.

53
00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:50.080
[George] Assuming powers had perished and that his self-destruct mechanism had obliterated the aircraft, the White House issued an elaborate cover story, claiming that a NASA weather research plane had accidentally strayed off course.

54
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] Then, in a theatrical trap, Khrushchev produced powers alive and confessed to espionage, displaying the intact wreckage and spy cameras to the world.

55
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:08.920
[George] Caught in a barefaced lie, Eisenhower refused to scapegoat his subordinates, stepping before the cameras to take sole personal responsibility.

56
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:20.120
[George] At the Paris summit, Khrushchev raged, demanded an apology, and stormed out, shattering Eisenhower's dream of concluding his presidency with a historic Cold War peace accord.

57
00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:32.760
[George] The public fallout from the U 2 incident cast a sombre shadow over Eisenhower's final months, demonstrating how covert espionage operations could inadvertently undermine delicate diplomatic breakthroughs.

58
00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:41.080
[George] Eisenhower's refusal to lie or shift blame after the U 2 was exposed reflected his lifelong standard of military accountability.

59
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.720
[George] The commander alone answers for the mission.

60
00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:50.600
[George] On the evening of January 17, 1961, three days before handing the presidency to John F.

61
00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:58.600
[George] Kennedy, 70-year-old Dwight Eisenhower sat at his desk in the Oval Office to deliver his farewell address to the American people.

62
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:07.400
[George] Unlike many departing executives who recite their achievements, Eisenhower issued a solemn, chilling prophecy that has echoed across generations.

63
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:18.840
[George] Acknowledging that the mortal threat of Soviet totalitarianism required a permanent defense establishment of unprecedented proportions, Eisenhower warned of an unintended domestic danger.

64
00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:28.920
[George] In the councils of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military industrial complex.

65
00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.960
[George] The potential for the disastrous rise of misplaced power exists and will persist.

66
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:48.240
[George] He cautioned that the immense confluence of military contractors, lobbying dollars and congressional self-interest could distort national priorities, drain the economic vitality of the republic, and subvert democratic accountability.

67
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:56.640
[George] He added a second, equally prescient warning that public policy could become captive to a scientific technological elite.

68
00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:03.840
[George] Coming from the supreme military conqueror of World War II, the warning was an unforgettable act of moral courage.

69
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:17.520
[George] His famous warning regarding the military-industrial complex remains the most quoted, debated, and revered farewell address delivered by an American president since George Washington stepped down in 1796.

70
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:27.680
[George] His prescient words served as a sober reminder that eternal vigilance is the price of liberty, particularly against the subtle creep of bureaucratic and corporate interests.

71
00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:40.800
[George] Eisenhower retired quietly with his beloved wife Mamie to their peaceful cattle farm in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, adjacent to the hallowed Civil War battlefield where Abraham Lincoln had consecrated the cemetery.

72
00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:58.480
[George] Over his eight years in the White House, Eisenhower had kept the United States out of war, balanced three federal budgets, presided over an era of low inflation and soaring living standards, and navigated the treacherous early years of the nuclear age with cool, calculating deterrence.

73
00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:09.680
[George] When intellectual critics during his presidency dismissed him as a passive golfer who bumbled through press conferences, they failed to recognize his calculating genius for hidden hand leadership.

74
00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:15.280
[George] Deliberately feigning vagueness in public to preserve his maneuvering room behind the scenes.

75
00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:26.080
[George] As historical archives opened in subsequent decades, Eisenhower was re-evaluated by scholars as one of the greatest and most effective chief executives in modern American history.

76
00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:32.680
[George] He passed away peacefully in March 1969 at Walter Reed Hospital at the age of 78.

77
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:36.040
[George] His life had mirrored the magnificent ascent of his country.

78
00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:45.000
[George] From a dirt farm in Kansas to the Supreme Command of Human Freedom, he embodied the highest ideals of Republican character and quiet strength.

79
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:58.360
[George] In retirement at Gettysburg, Eisenhower reflected that his greatest presidential achievement was eight years of peace, having guided the nation through nuclear peril without firing a single American soldier's weapon in combat.

80
00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:08.200
[George] Eisenhower demonstrated that true power resides in restraint, humility, and the quiet devotion to duty that seeks no applause but history's approval.

81
00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:09.640
[Rebecca] Dwight D.

82
00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:20.760
[Rebecca] Eisenhower left the American presidency, having preserved global peace, constructed the physical arteries of the modern nation, and warned against the perils of permanent militarism.

83
00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:27.160
[Rebecca] As the 1960s dawned, the torch was passed to a glamorous, youthful new generation.

84
00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:30.280
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the dazzling world of John F.

85
00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:44.520
[Rebecca] Kennedy, the legendary clan of Hyanisport, the harrowing midnight slicing of PT-109 in the Solomon Islands, the Coconut Shell that saved 11 lives, and the 1960 televised debates that captivated the world.

86
00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:48.840
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

